<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFrameworks>net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Version>0.1.0-preview.2</Version>
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>True</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>
		<DebugType>Embedded</DebugType>
		<Title>SpawnDev.Reachy.Browser</Title>
		<Authors>LostBeard</Authors>
		<Description>Drive a Reachy Mini from a browser over WebRTC, signalled through Hugging Face. A SpawnJS wrapper over @pollen-robotics/reachy-mini-sdk that implements SpawnDev.Reachy's IReachyMotion and IReachyLifecycle, so ReachyBody's gesture library, measured motion envelope and idle life run unchanged against a remote robot. This is the only route from a page served over HTTPS: the robot's daemon speaks plain HTTP on the LAN, which a secure page may not touch.</Description>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>0.1.0-preview.2 - the robot's own audio, both directions, verified on hardware. ReachySpeaker plays synthesised speech out of the robot's speaker, converting to the 16 kHz mono 16-bit WAV the daemon requires (it does not transcode, and the symptom of getting it wrong is silence or wrong-speed audio rather than an error); playback is daemon-side on the daemon's clock, so speech and motion do not drift apart over a wireless link. PlayTestToneAsync proves that path with no model in the way - reaching it through a real reply costs a language model and a cold voice load, and a failure anywhere in that chain looks identical to broken audio. ReachyEars exposes the robot's four-microphone array as a MediaStream for speech recognition; note that the SDK's own micStream is the OUTBOUND direction and defaults to a silent placeholder, and that the robot's media event fires once during the connect handshake - so the subscription must exist before connecting. VerifyHeadMatrixConventionAsync now homes first and samples a window, instead of taking one reading mid-travel and reporting a different layout each run.</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.Reachy</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.txt</PackageLicenseFile>
		<PackageIcon>icon-128.png</PackageIcon>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.Reachy.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageTags>Reachy;ReachyMini;Robotics;Robot;SDK;Pollen;Browser;WebRTC;SpawnJS;Blazor;WebAssembly</PackageTags>
		<NoPackageAnalysis>true</NoPackageAnalysis>
		<IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="..\icon-128.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\LICENSE.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- ProjectReference, not PackageReference: the two move together and this repo builds them both.
		     Consumers get SpawnDev.Reachy transitively at the version packed alongside. -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\SpawnDev.Reachy\SpawnDev.Reachy.csproj" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.SpawnJS" Version="2.1.17" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
